WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.256
(light whimsical music)

00:00:01.256 --> 00:00:02.089
- The world at the end

00:00:02.089 --> 00:00:04.537
of the decade will have
eight billion people.

00:00:04.537 --> 00:00:06.911
They'll be carrying 20
billion mobile devices,

00:00:06.911 --> 00:00:09.439
and we just want to
know how we can keep up

00:00:09.439 --> 00:00:11.427
with that huge amount of information,

00:00:11.427 --> 00:00:14.086
and rather than let someone
else think of the new thing,

00:00:14.086 --> 00:00:15.044
we thought what would it be like

00:00:15.044 --> 00:00:17.627
if we were to change the world.

00:00:21.054 --> 00:00:23.176
Moore's Law is actually
not a physical law,

00:00:23.176 --> 00:00:24.412
it's an observation.

00:00:24.412 --> 00:00:26.406
We take devices, in every generation,

00:00:26.406 --> 00:00:28.139
and we try and make them half the size

00:00:28.139 --> 00:00:30.605
that they were before.

00:00:30.605 --> 00:00:32.314
The observation, made by Gordon Moore,

00:00:32.314 --> 00:00:36.170
was that about every 18 to 24 months,

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:38.860
the number of transistors you could fit

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:42.106
into a given piece of
silicon semi-conductor

00:00:42.106 --> 00:00:44.522
was going to double.

00:00:44.522 --> 00:00:47.548
The industry has chased
this as that benchmark

00:00:47.548 --> 00:00:52.110
for continuous improvement
and that's gonna stop.

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:53.928
Things are gonna start to slow down

00:00:53.928 --> 00:00:55.972
on that Moore's Law of improvement

00:00:55.972 --> 00:00:57.913
because things are just getting too small

00:00:57.913 --> 00:00:59.113
to get any smaller.

00:00:59.113 --> 00:01:03.616
So, we find ourselves running
out of computation technology

00:01:03.616 --> 00:01:07.007
that has lifted our
productivity as an economy

00:01:07.007 --> 00:01:08.840
for at least 50 years.

00:01:09.985 --> 00:01:12.485
What if everything changed all at once?

00:01:12.485 --> 00:01:15.183
(light whimsical music)

00:01:15.183 --> 00:01:19.321
I'm overseeing a project
we call The Machine.

00:01:19.321 --> 00:01:20.908
The Machine will be far more powerful

00:01:20.908 --> 00:01:24.327
than today's computers and
consume far less energy.

00:01:24.327 --> 00:01:27.712
It will allow us to extract
insights from enormous data sets

00:01:27.712 --> 00:01:28.795
in real time.

00:01:30.556 --> 00:01:33.424
We'll actually have a tiny laser source

00:01:33.424 --> 00:01:36.660
and they'll be shooting
photons between each other

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:39.052
creating much greater
capacity for information,

00:01:39.052 --> 00:01:42.469
transmission at a much, much lower power.

00:01:44.894 --> 00:01:46.109
My name is Kirk Bresniker,

00:01:46.109 --> 00:01:49.550
and I'm a Fellow here at
Hewlett Packard Laboratories,

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:52.467
and I get to dream up crazy things,

00:01:53.801 --> 00:01:55.021
and say, "You know what?”

00:01:55.021 --> 00:01:57.451
"We're gonna do this."

00:01:57.451 --> 00:02:00.070
We want to show that we
have a really good shot

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:03.987
at surpassing the current
level of performance.

